"""Individual concept-specific figures and NCERT-style lessons for Chapter 4.2."""
from atlas_figures import shell
LESSONS=[{"title":"Blocking vs nonblocking I/O","text":"A blocking call keeps its calling thread waiting. With a nonblocking interface, the task can suspend the read and the execution thread can handle other ready work; completion is delivered later. The diagram tracks execution rather than promising improved CPU speed."},{"title":"Concurrency is not parallelism","text":"Concurrency means multiple tasks make progress over overlapping intervals. Parallelism means actual simultaneous execution. One core may switch between tasks, whereas separate cores may execute two tasks at the same instant."},{"title":"Follow a task through the event loop","text":"Suppose a document upload awaits storage. The event loop starts the task, registers its wait, and runs another ready task. When the operation completes, the first task becomes ready and can resume. A long CPU calculation would instead block this single event-loop thread."},{"title":"The bounded worker pool","text":"Work is accepted into a finite waiting queue and handled by a fixed number of workers. When all workers are busy and the queue is full, new work must wait upstream or be rejected according to an explicit policy. A queue limits admission; it does not create processing capacity."}]
